 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "The given figure shows the same graph colored in several ways. The largest clique of the graph has three vertices. Use this information to answer each question. \n<figure>\n<figcaption>Possible Colorings of the Same Graph</figcaption>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/c476d95c22a4c6fa.png\" alt=\"Three graphs. Graph 1 has 2 vertices in red, 2 vertices in blue, 1 vertex in purple, and 2 vertices in green. Edges from the first red vertex lead to two blue vertices, one green vertex, and one purple vertex. An edge from the second blue vertex leads to the purple vertex. Edges from the purple vertex lead to the second red and second green vertices. Graph 2 has 1 vertex in red, 3 vertices in blue, 2 vertices in green, and 1 vertex in purple. Edges from the red vertex lead to the green vertices, one blue vertex, and one purple vertex. The two green vertices are connected by an edge. The first blue connects with the purple via an edge. The purple connects with the other two blue vertices via edges. The second and third blue vertices are connected by an edge. Graph 3 has 1 red vertex, 2 orange vertexes, 1 green vertex, 2 blue vertices, and 1 purple vertex. Edges from the red vertex lead to orange, green, purple, and blue vertices. The first orange vertex connects with the green. The first blue connects with the first purple. This purple connects with the second orange and second blue vertices via edges. The second orange and the second blue are connected via an edge.\">\n</figure>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 542928,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Is the graph planar? What does your answer tell you about the chromatic number?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Yes, the graph is planar. A planar graph can be drawn on a flat surface so that no two edges are crossing. This graph has no edges crossing. The chromatic number of a planar graph is four or less."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Yes. The chromatic number is 4 or less."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542929,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What is the lowest possible chromatic number of this graph based on the size of any cliques?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "The graph has triangular cliques. The chromatic number of a graph is at least the number of vertices in its biggest cliques. Thus, the chromatic number of this graph is at least 3."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "3"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542930,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What are the possible chromatic numbers?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Because it is planar, the chromatic number is 4 or less. Because the largest clique is a triangle, the chromatic number is at least 3. Putting this information together, the chromatic number is either 3 or 4."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "3 or 4"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542931,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which graph or graphs meet the definition of a valid graph coloring?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Graphs 1 and 3 are valid. Graph 2 is not since it has two adjacent vertices with the same color. Graph 3 is valid even though it uses more colors than it needs to use."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Graphs 1 and 3"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 542932,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Draw an <span data-math=\"n\">n</span>-coloring for the graph where <span data-math=\"n\">n</span> is the answer to Exercise 2.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Answers may vary. Here is an example of a 3-coloring:\n<br>\n<img src=\"https://exercises.openstax.org/rails/active_storage/blobs/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsibWVzc2FnZSI6IkJBaHBBbFVQIiwiZXhwIjpudWxsLCJwdXIiOiJibG9iX2lkIn19--2c258f9034c61095686b97623268d0a6f7aa8ee8/CS_Figure_12_04_121.png\" alt=\"A graph with seven vertices. It has 2 vertices in red, 3 vertices in blue, and 2 vertices in purple. Edges from the first red vertex lead to two blue vertices and two purple vertices. The first purple and the first blue vertices are connected by an edge. The second purple and the second blue vertices are connected by an edge. The second purple connects with the second red and third blue via edges. The second red and third blue are connected via an edge.\">"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
